1. Introduction
The gas atomization process in powder metallurgy has been described as the disintegration of a liquid mass into a collection of liquid metal droplets by the impact of high velocity gas flow. The liquid metal droplets subsequently spheroidize, cool and solidify into metal powder particles, which can typically range from 1 m to 1 mm in diameter. The spherical metal alloy powders produced by the gas atomization process have very attractive material properties because of the inherent high cooling rate of this process. Furthermore, the spherical powder morphology is also very desirable for the growing metal injection molding industry. One of the main advantages of the gas atomization process is that it can produce many solidification benefits such as particle microstructure refinement, which is unattainable by conventional ingot metallurgy. This refinement is particularly beneficial to highly alloyed materials
such as tool steels and nickel-based superalloys, where the alloy microstructure has tremendous influence on the material properties. In a typical gas atomization process, the molten metal stream flows from the bottom of a heated tundish through a melt feed tube into the atomization zone. Here, the liquid is disintegrated into a collection of liquid droplets as it encounters a high velocity gas stream just below the exit of the melt orifice. Depending on the particular design, there are generally two types of gas atomization configurations that are used in low and moderate pressure powder metallurgy production. These are known as: (1) open-stream or free-fall; and (2) closed, confined-feed, or close-coupled atomization [1]. For applications where fine powder yields are not necessary, a free-fall melt configuration is adequate. In this situation, the unconstrained stream of molten metal falls under the influence of gravity from the outlet of the tundish through the atomizing gas. Due to the absence of liquid melt stability and concentricity, the free-fall gas atomization configuration is unsuitable as an efficient gas atomization technique. On the other
