hand, the confined-feed gas atomization, where the gas jets impinge on the pour tube prior to merging at a focal point located in front of the orifice of the pour tube, provides for a more efficient configuration to transfer the kinetic energy of the atomizing gas to the melt stream for producing finer powders. Therefore, the desire to achieve a greater yield of ultra-fine powders (diameter 20 m) with improved efficiency at high to moderate gas pressure seems to promote the use of confined-feed atomization nozzle in powder metal production industries [1– 7]. Even though the confined-feed nozzles are prevalent in powder metal production industries and are acknowledged as the most efficient gas atomization configuration for fine powder production, the operation of a confined-feed nozzle is still generally regarded as a difficult ‘art’. A desired operational characteristic of the confined-feed nozzle configuration is that subambient pressure near the melt orifice is needed for stable atomization. The rapidly flowing gas passing the periphery of the melt tip can produce an overambient or subambient pressure region in front of the delivery tube orifice that is often known as the aspiration base pressure. Melt freeze-off can arise in high pressure gas atomization, when the aspiration base pressure is near or above ambient pressure at the melt pour tube orifice. Under such high pressure conditions, the stability at the melt tip can better be controlled, if the atomizer nozzle and melt tip are designed to experience a subambient pressure. Operating in this intrinsic aspiration regime, the subambient pressure at the orifice should provide for a more stable melt feed by drawing the melt stream towards the atomization zone. Consequently, the melt orifice aspiration base pressures have generally been used as an indicator for evaluating the atomization stability of a gravity-fed, confined-feed atomizer nozzle. The majority of the nozzles currently used in producing metal powders belong to the High Pressure Gas Atomizer (HPGA) type nozzles with an annular-slit or discrete jets. Numerous researchers have indicated that atomizing at the deep gas-aspiration pressures (at condition of wake-closure) in close coupled gas atomizers yields the finest atomized powder particle distribution [2– 6]. On the other hand, Miller et al. concluded that the atomization performance is, at best, weakly related to aspiration [7]. But none has provided a satisfactory model that could explain these observations. The causes and influences of the wake-closure are investigated and presented here in an pulsatile atomization model that could readily explain many of the atomization observations described in this work and elsewhere. The relationship between measured deep aspiration generated by an industrial gas atomizer nozzle and melt flow rate with observed particle size distribution will be given.
2. Experimental
Seven 22.7 kg heats of a Ni-based alloy were atomized. Prior to atomizing each heat, the gas-only aspiration profile of the atomizer nozzle was measured with a digital pressure transducer to determine the manifold pressure at which wake-closure occurred. The digital data were acquired through a data logger and stored in a portable laptop PC. Two pour tube set lengths, which represent the length of the pour tube protrusion from the base of the atomizer nozzle, were investigated in this study. The aspiration base pressure for the shorter pour tube can be seen in Fig. 1, and that for the longer pour tube can be seen in Fig. 2. The aspiration measurements from the atomizer nozzle with the longer pour tube indicated that the wake-closure pressure (hereafter known as WCP) developed around 4.93–4.95 MPa (715–718 psi); this was highly repeatable. In this study, only the atomizer nozzle with the longer pour tube configuration will be used in atomization heats. For each atomization heat of the Ni-based alloys, the atomization gas pressure in the nozzle manifold was selectively tuned to atomize at pressures either above, or below the WCP. Heats that were atomized below WCP had shallow gas-only aspiration of 0.25 atm (3.7 psi) below atmosphere pressure, while heats atomized above WCP had deep gas-only aspiration of 0.58 atm (8.5 psi) below atmosphere. The atomization heats were timed. The atomization pressure was monitored, and the particle size distribution was vibratory screened and sonically sieved to obtain the particle size distribution above and below 45 m, respectively.
3. Results
The particle size distributions from this study are given in Fig. 3 and the atomization results are tabulated in Table 1. From the table, one found that the heats that are atomized at pressures above WCP produced powders that have dramatically smaller median particle size than those heats atomized at pressures below WCP (Fig. 3). In particular, the median particle size from L1270 that was atomized 0.017 MPa above WCP was 42% (or, 26.5 micron) smaller compared with that for L1271 that was atomized 0.034 MPa below WCP. This particle refinement in L1270 is a result of the heat having a 60% longer atomization time, translating to a 64% greater GMR, than L1271 (Fig. 4). In addition, atomizing at pressures just above WCP also was associated with a 23% improvement in particle size standard deviation (D84/D50) compared with that atomized below WCP (Fig. 5). Atomizing above WCP gave a narrower particle size distribution. Seeing that the gas atomization pressures for both cases differed by less than 1.5%, the observed improvement in particle refinement cannot


