 WCP, is only ‘fed’ by a weak stagnation pressure at point 2, which in terms causes one to detect a deep aspiration pressure at WCP condition. The presence of an aspiration hysteresis in this study not only reveals a region of atomization instability, but it also indicates that the creation of the wake closure phenomenon requires the atomizing gas overcoming an energy barrier — in another word, an energy of formation for the wake closure. The argument for the presence of an energy barrier, that the atomizing gas flow has to over come, is further varified by the fact that the wake closure pressure is very abrupt at long pour tube length. The abruptness of the onset of wake closure and the absence of a wide hysteresis behavior only demonstrates that at high atomizing pressure the atomizing gas energy closely matched the energy of formation. Therefore, a gradual transition to establish wake-closure is absent. On the other hand, at low wake closure pressure that occurs with short pour tube length, once
the take closure is formed during manifold pressure ramp-up, in order to overcome the energy barrier during manifold pressure ramp-down, the gas flow experiences the same energy barrier necessitating the break up the wake closure features. The wake closure can be seen stable at 0.25 MPa below the first wake closure. The hysteresis in the low WCP condition suggests that the wake-closure formation energy, including the establishment of a Mach disk with attached oblique shocks, can be greater than the kinetic energy of the atomizing gas. For example, in order to transition from open-wake to closed-wake at WCP, the atomizing gas has to reverse the flow of the recirculating gas in the wake, and then has to re-accelerate the reversed gas flow to supersonic velocity from point 2 (Fig. 9) to lead to the formation of the Mach disk down stream.
3.2. Atomization model
In gas-only flow conditions during aspiration measurements, the gas flow field surrounding the nozzle accommodates itself via dynamic gas compression and expansion to modulate the pressure between the high velocity gas and the ambient surroundings. Introducing liquid metal into the wake, however, during atomization upsets the gas equilibrium. The presence of incompressible liquid in the wake acts against the compressible gas, disturbing and displacing the established gas dynamic flow features. Even though most close-coupled atomization practices initiates the flow of liquid metal before the atomizing gas, one will describe the atomization process commencing with a established gas flow field for the sake of clarity. Albeit, the initial melt flow is only a transient state in the whole atomization process. When initiating liquid atomization at a pressure just below WCP the melt is drawn into the wake by the shallow aspiration in the open wake configuration. This is depicted in Fig. 10a–b showing the melt transition. As more melt flows into the wake, the physical boundary of the melt asserts itself on the external gas flow that opens-up the wake region further (see Fig. 10c). In this new ‘opened’-wake configuration, more gas can enter the wake via the enlarged stagnation front, leading to an increase in recirculation gas pressure that could retard the melt flow. As the melt flow slows, the physical boundary asserted by the melt on the gas is momentarily removed, and the wake geometry returns to its original shape that is depicted in Fig. 10b. Then the earlier discussed behaviors repeat themselves. In state 10-c, the recirculation gas may be stronger than that of in state 10-a or 10-b, slowing the melt flow; while in state 10-b the recirculation gas is weak, encouraging aspiration and increasing the melt flow. Due to these changes in gas dynamics, the recirculating gas acts as melt feed control, and the atomization process oscil


