oscillation between ‘opened’ and closed-wake scenarios repeat themselves rapidly, leading to what one often observes during melt atomization as a steady pulsation in melt intensity, having fluctuating color and brightness.
4. Discussion
The oscillating behavior hypothesized in the above atomization model indicates that pulsatile atomization mechanism is possibly an inherent feature found in all melt atomization processes. The melt behaves only as a temporary and dynamic solid boundary that adjusts itself continuously during the atomization. From this reasoning, the design for non-oscillating (or quiescent) atomization would be impossible. Several studies have indicated that the flow of liquid metal during atomization may possess a pulsatile characteristic [14–16]. Due to our ability to visually notice the pulsatile behavior during the atomization process suggests that the pulsating frequency has to be much less than 60 Hz. In general, events occurring greater than 60 Hz can not be resolved by the human eye. In high speed film study of rocket fuel atomization, however, Glogowski et al. [14] observed regions of high and low droplet density occurring at wave-like frequency. This observation further substantiates our hypothesized pulsatile atomization mechanism.


