The Mach disk formed at WCP, also known as the ‘barrel’ shock, is an intricate equilibrium gas flow feature. The name ‘barrel’ shock is coined from its shape resembling a wine barrel. This shape can also be observed in discrete convergent jets gas flow field [17,18]. The equilibrium state can be readily observed in the short pour tube aspiration measurement (see Fig. 1). The fact that the wake-closure can exists at a lower WCP during manifold pressure ramp-down, suggests that wake-closure is a stable equilibrium gas feature. Undoubtedly, the creation of the normal Mach disk, oblique shocks, and reflective shocks require significant energy. Concomitantly, however, is that the entropy of the system increases, resulting from the supersonic gas crossing the Mach disk and oblique shocks that decelerate the supersonic gas before the shock to subsonic velocity after the shocks. This is necessary to accommodate the rapid rise in pressure and temperature across
