the atomization plume, while the majority of the atomization occurred in the centerline during nonresonant mode. This reveals that the melt may enter the wake in two distinct forms during atomization: (1) predominantly as a solid melt core when atomizing below WCP; or (2) principally as a melt film on the melt tip when atomizing above WCP. These two distinct forms are of course highly dependent upon density, flow rate, viscosity and surfaced tension of the melt, and its interaction with upward flowing gas in the wake. Therefore, two distinct atomization mechanisms may be occurring at any one time during atomization depending on the operating pressure of the atomizing nozzle. Atomizing at pressures below WCP, the atomization is dominated by the secondary melt breakup mechanism, where the solid melt core entering the wake is atomized as if it was experiencing pseudo-free-fall atomization by direct gas impact. The difference in this pseudo-free-fall atomization mechanism compared to the traditional free-fall atomization, is that the gas is brought geometrically closer to the melt core for intensified melt disintegration. Atomizing at pressures above WCP, the atomization is assisted by the primary melt breakup mechanism known to be melt filming. This dominant melt disintegration mechanism above WCP is supplemented by the secondary breakup mechanism that occurs outside the wake down stream. This melt filming breakup mechanism is most likely to have occurred as the sheet-to-ligament break-up disintegration process suggested by Dombrowski and Johns [24]. As it was demonstrated in this study, Heat L1271, which was atomized above WCP, generated a narrower particle size distribution than Heat L1270, which was atomized below WCP, even though both Heats were atomized at pressures that were within 1.4% of one another.
5. Conclusions
Using molten metal atomization, this study has demonstrated that deep aspiration (above wake-closure pressure) does not translate into an increase in molten metal flow rate. On the contrary, WCP condition slows down the melt flow. Therefore, atomization rates can not be related to the depth of the gas-only aspiration pressures. This study confirmed the work by Miller et al. [7] who used water atomization to show that there is little correlation between aspiration pressure and liquid flow rate. Atomizing at above WCP indeed produce the finest powder but this is achieved at the expense of higher GMR compared with below WCP atomization. This study has demonstrated experimentally that atomizing at pressures just 0.014 MPa (2 psi) above the WCP increases the yield of finer particles, compared to yields obtained at 0.034 MPa (5 psi) below WCP by 42% or by 26.5 microns.
The pressure at which wake-closure occurs around 4.94 MPa (715 psi) in the industrial atomizer nozzle is highly repeatable. The occurrence of wake-closure, of course, is highly specific to the nozzle designs and setups. In the short pour tube configuration, two wake closures were observed at 4.35 and 4.1 MPa, during manifold pressure ramp-up and ramp-down, respectively. Thus suggesting that the wake closure phenomenon is an equilibrium gas flow feature that has an energy of formation. A gas dynamic model is presented describing the formation of wake closure during deep aspiration. The model showed that a reduced recirculation wake size corresponds to a weaker recirculation strength, which would give rise to the deep aspiration pressure measurement that one has observed in the study. A pulsating atomization model is presented using gas dynamics to explain the mechanics of the atomization processes occurring above and below WCP. The model shows that stable wake-closure cannot be sustained during melt atomization. And that the stagnation front pressure at wake-closure facilitates melt filming during the ‘opened’-wake state of the pulsatile atomization process. This melt filming process at above WCP gives rises to finer particle size distribution, lower melt flow rate, and higher GMR. On the other hand, operating below WCP, the melt breakup mechanism could be similar to that of the free-fall atomization process, where the melt is introduced into the wake as a solid core and it is atomized mostly by impact collision. This process at below WCP is less efficient than melt filming at above WCP and therefore gave rise to larger mean particle sizes and wider standard deviations. This study has presented a model that has elucidated the causes of why one atomizer condition yielded finer particle size distribution when operating above WCP compared to yields obtained when atomizing below WCP, even though the two atomization pressures may be different by less than 1.5% of each other. Finally, this paper has presented a hypothesis for a pulsatile atomization model that may have elucidated the association between the measured gas-only aspiration pressures and its effects and influences on the powder yields obtained in the melt atomizations operated around WCP.
